**Optimization findings**

- **Shadow price of the capital budget K: {{phase4.shadow_price_capital|.3f}}.** K appears in two rows, the C-1 charge ≤ K and the post-downgrade charge ≤ {{inputs.downgrade_buffer_multiple|.2f}}·K. So dIncome/dK = dual_capital + {{inputs.downgrade_buffer_multiple|.2f}} × dual_buffer. At the base budget, one more dollar of K adds about ${{phase4.shadow_price_capital|.2f}} a year of net spread. The value is {{phase4.frontier_shadow_at_min_budget|.2f}} at K = ${{inputs.frontier.min|.2f}}M and 0 once capital used reaches ${{phase4.frontier_capital_at_max_budget|.2f}}M, where the frontier tops out at {{phase4.frontier_income_at_max_budget|.3f}} and the HY, CLO and issuer limits bind instead. At the base budget the binding capital row is {{report.binding_capital.text}} (dual_capital {{phase6.constraints.dual_capital|.4f}}, dual_buffer {{phase6.constraints.dual_downgrade_buffer|.4f}}).
- **Why the LP does not swap more BBB into HY.** A ${{phase4.swap_bbb_to_bbplus.amount|.0f}}M BBB → BB+ swap earns {{phase4.swap_bbb_to_bbplus.income_gain|.2%}} of spread for {{phase4.swap_bbb_to_bbplus.capital_change|.2%}} of capital, but the HY (NAIC 3+) limit is binding with a dual of {{phase4.swap_bbb_to_bbplus.hy_max_dual|.4f}}. Priced at all binding duals, the swap's reduced cost is {{phase4.swap_bbb_to_bbplus.reduced_cost|.1e}}.
- **CLO factor regime.**
  - (a) C's holdings repriced on the old bond factors need ${{phase4.clo_regime.C_capital_old_bond_same_holdings|.3f}}M of capital, versus ${{phase4.clo_regime.C_capital_new_2026|.3f}}M under the 2026 CLO factors.
  - (b) Re-optimized on the old factors, the LP buys more CLO BB ({{phase4.clo_bb_new_2026|.2f}} → {{phase4.clo_bb_old_bond|.2f}}) and earns {{phase4.clo_regime.reoptimized_old_bond_income|.3f}} using ${{phase4.clo_regime.reoptimized_old_bond_capital|.3f}}M of capital.
  - The 2026 factors raise BBB tranches to {{inputs.factors.clo_2B|.3%}} (from {{inputs.factors.bond_2B|.3%}}) and BB tranches to {{inputs.factors.clo_3B|.3%}} (from {{inputs.factors.bond_3B|.3%}}).
- **Robustness and the optimizer's curse.** Over {{phase5.robustness.n_draws}} draws (spreads × N(1, {{inputs.robustness.spread_sd|.2f}}), PDs × mean-one lognormal with σ = {{inputs.robustness.pd_sigma|.2f}}):

  | | Mean income |
  |---|---|
  | Re-optimized, in-sample | {{phase5.robustness.income_reopt_in_sample_mean|.3f}} |
  | Base C held fixed, same draws | {{phase5.robustness.income_fixed_C_mean|.3f}} |
  | Re-optimized, out-of-sample | {{phase5.robustness.income_reopt_out_of_sample_mean|.3f}} |

  - The in-sample gap of {{phase5.robustness.optimizers_curse_gap|+.4f}} is the optimizer's curse.
  - Out-of-sample, re-optimized portfolios minus fixed C: {{phase5.robustness.oos_minus_fixed_C|+.4f}}.

C does not win every metric. A has the best capital efficiency and gains when rates fall, and B earns slightly more income by breaking the capital and rate limits. C is the only portfolio inside every limit, and it pays for that with a short-duration position ({{phase5.stress.C_parallel_down_100_dS|+.2f}} at −100bp) and more spread risk.

## Scenario constraints and downgrade buffer

The duration and KRD rows are linear, so they cannot see negative convexity. The model adds one exact, still linear row per curve scenario. Each row uses the full-repricing value change per dollar of every security, with cash flows re-projected on the shocked curve:

> ΔA_s(x) − ΔL_s ≥ −{{inputs.scenario_max_loss|.2f}} for s ∈ {+100bp, −100bp, steepener, flattener}

It also adds a **downgrade buffer**: capital after a one-notch BBB-family downgrade ≤ {{inputs.downgrade_buffer_multiple|.2f}} × K. Both are switchable in `config/policy.yaml`. A test checks that the LP rows reproduce the stress engine's full-repricing ΔS exactly.

| Portfolio C | Before | After |
|---|---|---|
| Net spread income per year | {{phase6.constraints.income_before|.4f}} | {{phase6.constraints.income_after|.4f}} |
| Δ surplus, −100bp | {{phase6.constraints.dS_parallel_down_100_C_before|+.3f}} | {{phase6.constraints.dS_parallel_down_100_C_after|+.3f}} |
| Δ surplus, +100bp | {{phase6.constraints.dS_parallel_up_100_C_before|+.3f}} | {{phase6.constraints.dS_parallel_up_100_C_after|+.3f}} |
| Δ surplus, combined adverse (B: {{phase6.constraints.dS_combined_adverse_B|+.3f}}) | {{phase6.constraints.dS_combined_adverse_C_before|+.3f}} | {{phase6.constraints.dS_combined_adverse_C_after|+.3f}} |
| Surplus DV01 ($k per bp) | {{phase6.constraints.surplus_dv01_before|+.2f}} | {{phase6.constraints.surplus_dv01_after|+.2f}} |
| Capital after one-notch BBB downgrade (limit {{report.downgrade_buffer_limit|.2f}}) | {{phase6.constraints.capital_downgraded_before|.3f}} | {{phase6.constraints.capital_downgraded_after|.3f}} |

- **Binding scenario:** −100bp, with a dual of {{phase6.constraints.dual_scenario_parallel_down_100|.4f}}. Each extra dollar of allowed −100bp loss is worth about that much income a year.
- **Downgrade buffer:** {{report.binding_capital.buffer_status}}, with a dual of {{phase6.constraints.dual_downgrade_buffer|.4f}}.
- **Costs in income:** the scenario rows cost {{phase6.constraints.scenario_cost|.4f}} a year, and the downgrade buffer a further {{phase6.constraints.buffer_cost|.4f}}.
- **Combined adverse:** before the constraints C lost more than B; after them it loses less.
- **Optional HY-count row** (off by default): NAIC 3+ holdings after the same one-notch BBB downgrade ≤ {{inputs.limits.hy_max|.0f}}. Switching it on cuts income to {{phase6.constraints.income_with_hy_after_downgrade_limit|.4f}}, a cost of {{phase6.constraints.hy_after_downgrade_limit_cost|.4f}} a year. It forces BBB− names out, because each of them becomes 3.A.

**The swap overlay with scenario constraints on.** Receivers make −100bp protection almost free:

| Allowed −100bp loss | Income, cash bonds only | Income, with overlay |
|---|---|---|
| {{report.sweep.loosest_limit|.2f}} | {{report.sweep.loosest_no_overlay|.4f}} | {{report.sweep.loosest_with_overlay|.4f}} |
| {{report.sweep.tightest_limit|.2f}} | {{report.sweep.tightest_no_overlay|.4f}} | {{report.sweep.tightest_with_overlay|.4f}} |

- Tightening the −100bp loss limit from the loosest to the tightest value costs {{headline.cost_to_zero_down_100_loss_no_overlay|.4f}} a year with cash bonds, but only {{headline.cost_to_zero_down_100_loss_with_overlay|.4f}} with the overlay.
- At the base limit, the −100bp dual falls from {{phase6.constraints.dual_scenario_parallel_down_100|.4f}} to {{phase6.overlay.dual_parallel_down_C_swaps|.4f}}.

